{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774731,"nonce":"7xqHBdDvGZwuXJiPsK+Ew8iyumU=","title":"test(db): Db port contract suite — fake/real parity (test-strategy Phase 1)","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #64 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `test/phase1-db-contract` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-24T03:43:01Z · Merged: 2026-07-24T05:48:27Z\n\nImplements Phase 1.4 of the test strategy (`docs/audits/2026-07-24-test-strategy.md` §Phase 1, PR #60): a shared contract suite proving `InMemoryDb` and `PostgresDb` agree on the `Db` port semantics the engine relies on, so fake drift (the eae99d6/b387cd5 class of hand-mirrored fixes) is caught at `cargo test` time.\n\n\n`crates/scarab-db-postgres/tests/db_contract.rs` — six contracts, each a generic async fn over `\u0026dyn Db`, invoked twice via a small macro: once against `InMemoryDb` (unconditional) and once against a throwaway real-PG database (existing `fresh_db()` pattern; skips cleanly when `SCARAB_TEST_DATABASE_URL` is unset).\n\n1. **Transition OCC** — two competing run/step transitions from the same observed version: exactly one wins, the loser gets a clean `Conflict`, stale replays are rejected (no lost update, no double-apply).\n2. **`record_attempt` idempotency** — recording the same attempt twice is a no-op (`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` mirror).\n3. **Evidence never downgrades** — a re-driven `record_attempt` cannot reset a recorded `Failed` verdict; `Superseded`/`Cancelled` survive later failure/outcome writes.\n4. **Lease semantics** — grant; no takeover while held; the holder's renewal **extends**; takeover only after expiry.\n5. **Outbox** — exactly-once enqueue on the idempotency key; a claim hides the message for its visibility window; an **expired claim is redelivered**; kind-scoped claims don't steal foreign work; monotonic failure counter; a dead-lettered message is never claimed again.\n6. **Attempt-ordering determinism** — `started_at`, then numeric `a{n}` suffix (`a2` \u003c `a10`), via both `attempts_of_step` and `steps_of_run`.\n\n\n- **Postgres lease renewal did not extend `expires_at`** — the `DO UPDATE` only fired on an expired lease, so a constantly-renewing scheduler leader still expired after one TTL and a peer could steal leadership at the boundary. The fake and the port doc had the intended semantic; added the `owner = EXCLUDED.owner` arm to the guard.\n- **The fake's outbox claim expiry was reader-side** — reclaimability depended on the *reclaiming* call's `visibility_ms \u003c= 0`, not the stored claim's window (postgres `claimed_until`). A crashed drainer's expired claim was not redelivered to a positive-visibility drainer in the fake. The fake now stores the claim lease per row; vis-0 drivers behave exactly as before.\n\nBoth failures were verified red before the fixes and green after (each on its correct side: `lease_semantics::postgres`, `outbox_poison::in_memory`).\n\n\nThe strategy doc sketches contract suites next to the ForgePort one in `scarab-testkit`; this suite lives in **`scarab-db-postgres`** instead, because testkit dev-depending on db-postgres would create a dependency cycle (db-postgres tests already dev-depend on testkit).\n\n\n- Lease expiry in both stores is wall-clock (`now()` / `Instant`), not the `Clock` port, so the lease contract uses short real TTLs + sleeps rather than `FakeClock` (noted in the file header).\n- The Db-side poison semantic is the monotonic failure counter + never-reclaim-after-dead-letter; the bound itself (`MAX_DELIVERY_ATTEMPTS`) is scheduler policy and stays covered by `tests/poison.rs`.\n\n\n- `cargo test -p scarab-db-postgres --test db_contract` — 12/12 green, both without PG (6 skips) and against a real Postgres 16 (compose stack, all 12 executed).\n- Full PG-enabled `scarab-db-postgres` + `scarab-testkit` suites: 123 passed.\n- `just test` (workspace): green.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}